#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133495 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ae774f-2496-56c0-a800-450bd8383092" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Inner Product of Two Complex Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "3263f466-0901-58a7-8cd9-ed62cd3036a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5545" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb C^3,$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle$ of the following two vectors.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057166/assets/5d0567f732027d096d674c2513a41a9c32b87c78bb0203d60653d555c8c9a3b9.png)" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}} \\\\  & =i\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-3})+(-2)\\cdot \\overset{―}{4+i}+0\\cdot \\overset{―}{5} \\\\  & =i\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot (4-i)+0 \\\\  & =-3i-8+2i \\\\  & =-8-i.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133496 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/id #uuid "b30bd921-7418-5a57-96eb-0c79dd5e4712" :question/math-academy-id "e-5547" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $\\langle \\,\\cdot\\,, \\,\\cdot\\, \\rangle$ over $\\mathbb{C}.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle\\in\\mathbb R$ for any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$\n\n- $\\langle \\mathrm{i}\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle =  \\langle \\mathbf{a},  -\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$ for any  $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$\n\n- $\\langle\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a},\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number for any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\alpha \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\alpha \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\alpha\\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\alpha} \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number, and $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = 0$ if and only if $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the linearity in the first argument  and the antilinearity in the second argument, we have\n\n$$\n\\langle  \\mathrm{i}\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle =  \\mathrm{i}\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle  = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\overline{\\, \\mathrm{i}\\, } \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a},- \\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{b} \\rangle\n$$\n\n \nfor any  $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, by the linearity in the first argument  and the antilinearity in the second argument, we have\n\n$$\n\\langle\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a},\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a} \\rangle =\\mathrm{i}\\cdot \\bar{ \\mathrm{i} }\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = -\\mathrm{i}^2\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle= \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle\n$$\n\n \nfor any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n.$ But by positive-definiteness, this is a nonnegative real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133604 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/id #uuid "8d5f83f0-dfb9-5437-a0e5-f33f68a59cec" :question/math-academy-id "e-8578" :question/problem "Given that $\\langle \\,\\cdot\\,, \\,\\cdot\\, \\rangle$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $\\mathbb{C},$ find $\\big\\langle -2\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}, \\: \\mathbf{c} \\big\\rangle$ if \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = 3+5\\text{i}, \\quad \\quad \\langle \\mathbf{c}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = 2-4\\text{i}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\alpha \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\alpha \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\alpha\\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\alpha} \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number, and $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = 0$ if and only if $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨-2a+3b,\\,c⟩ & =⟨-2a,c⟩+⟨3b,c⟩ \\\\  & =-2⟨a,c⟩+3⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2⟨a,c⟩+3⟨b,c⟩ & =-2⟨a,c⟩+3\\overset{―}{⟨c,b⟩}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle$ and $\\langle \\mathbf{c}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2⟨a,c⟩+3\\overset{―}{⟨c,b⟩} & =-2(3+5\\text{i})+3\\overset{―}{(2-4\\text{i})} \\\\  & =-2(3+5\\text{i})+3(2+4\\text{i}) \\\\  & =-6-10\\text{i}+6+12\\text{i} \\\\  & =2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "181d6878-54c8-523b-9328-0b0a31098a4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334261} {:answer/id #uuid "524ac6b3-2e16-535b-86bb-1a3a744dd3ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334262} {:answer/id #uuid "617a3510-3ce9-54a9-b908-273b7d5e09b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334263} {:answer/id #uuid "ae80f021-02b8-513c-87f2-99fa175a06c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334264} {:answer/id #uuid "0c9e4dc7-12a7-5d5c-81c5-b582ddd29819" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae80f021-02b8-513c-87f2-99fa175a06c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334264} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5685e55-d64b-5b7b-8a58-02ad62c79e1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "91ad2ee8-eb6d-5323-a363-48d5ab23b581" :question/math-academy-id "q-141160" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨ia,b⟩=i⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=⟨\\alpha a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n- $⟨ia,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true.  Indeed, the inner product in ${C}^{n}$ is linear in the first argument. So, a constant multiple from the first argument can be factored out.\n\n- Statement II is true. By the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument and linearity in the first argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=\\overset{―}{(\\overset{―}{\\alpha })}⟨a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩=⟨\\alpha a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}.$ Therefore, if $a\\ne 0,$ then\n\n$$\n⟨ia,a⟩=i⟨a,a⟩\\notin R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ffdcda1-c24e-51e4-943d-d5f7ff7f37a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8+7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334268} {:answer/id #uuid "21a46b06-586d-5437-aaf9-1bb3455accfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334269} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb4f2a1-62fb-51f0-a87d-b802ece00745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7-4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334270} {:answer/id #uuid "ac56175f-02af-5936-8661-96f49b5d8a21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7+8\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334271} {:answer/id #uuid "d5db2c9e-ec3c-5cd5-95a7-d523056e0a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7+4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac56175f-02af-5936-8661-96f49b5d8a21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7+8\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334271} :answer-field/id #uuid "50a2b0a5-dc01-5c4b-be7f-8e93bb04a234" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0f3a852-f21d-5ba1-9973-db675afd165e" :question/math-academy-id "q-141161" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨2a+3b,\\,c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨c,a⟩=2-\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨b,c⟩=1+2\\text{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨2a+3b,\\,c⟩ & =⟨2a,c⟩+⟨3b,c⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨a,c⟩+3⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2⟨a,c⟩+3⟨b,c⟩ & =2\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}+3⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨c,a⟩$ and $⟨b,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}+3⟨b,c⟩ & =2(\\overset{―}{2-\\text{i}})+3(1+2\\text{i}) \\\\  & =2(2+\\text{i})+3(1+2\\text{i}) \\\\  & =4+2\\text{i}+3+6\\text{i} \\\\  & =7+8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aab14461-fafd-5ce8-a827-b7fbcd8ba123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4+8\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333366} {:answer/id #uuid "632d4c5b-412e-5d54-8287-deafd0eb4f50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333367} {:answer/id #uuid "bbb1f13b-8ee6-5ffa-a10a-2c6c4e3eeb9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11+8\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333368} {:answer/id #uuid "5673cb0f-58ae-5230-9bed-341314357b1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4+11\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333369} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a10379-ceca-5e6e-8aa5-185c292e236c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7+6\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5673cb0f-58ae-5230-9bed-341314357b1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4+11\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333369} :answer-field/id #uuid "bccea3f0-485f-5dae-94e4-fcbdad249dfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0f409f0a-d1f3-5a6f-b4c4-c490f83517f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-141162" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨a,\\,2b+(3-2\\text{i})c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨b,a⟩=1-\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,c⟩=3\\text{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is antilinear in the second argument:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,\\,2b+(3-2\\text{i})c⟩ & =⟨a,2b⟩+⟨a,(3-2\\text{i})c⟩ \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{2}⟨a,b⟩+(\\overset{―}{3-2\\text{i}})⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨a,b⟩+(3+2\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2⟨a,b⟩+(3+2\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩ & =2\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+(3+2\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨b,a⟩$ and $⟨a,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+(3+2\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩ & =2\\cdot \\overset{―}{1-\\text{i}}+(3+2\\text{i})\\cdot (3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (1+\\text{i})-6+9\\text{i} \\\\  & =-4+11\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ae774f-2496-56c0-a800-450bd8383092" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Inner Product of Two Complex Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d304c970-df1a-5b57-b5df-7de4eb02a4d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334275} {:answer/id #uuid "3c1e1203-7d74-5261-9020-04606cecde28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334276} {:answer/id #uuid "47f28c12-51f6-512a-989c-7c0f5307b882" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334277} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d8e6d8-5687-51cf-a37c-60ea2aa91686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334278} {:answer/id #uuid "1455a7ef-98c3-5f7c-8897-8a7b59c49375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c1e1203-7d74-5261-9020-04606cecde28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334276} :answer-field/id #uuid "61d68cb4-9ae0-52c9-984b-7dd175baa217" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3589bf59-3ac6-5c59-a90f-136e4c840a92" :question/math-academy-id "q-43806" :question/problem "Given that $x,y\\in {C}^{4},$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x,y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2i \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -i \\\\ 1 \\\\ i\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}}+{x}_{4}\\overset{―}{{y}_{4}} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\overset{―}{2}+1\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-i})+2i\\cdot \\overset{―}{1}+3\\cdot \\overset{―}{\\,\\text{i}} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot i+2i\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =2+i+2i-3\\text{i} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ae774f-2496-56c0-a800-450bd8383092" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Inner Product of Two Complex Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d1889d6-128e-5736-8a4e-4ea5163121ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334282} {:answer/id #uuid "13af33f6-be1c-5e55-bf2e-a5dd0b5c6968" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334283} {:answer/id #uuid "407c5bdd-9225-517e-8bec-5a6c0aad2529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334284} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d157c1-7967-52f9-9c02-0c3169f14907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334285} {:answer/id #uuid "7f66f5f6-ec00-531e-8625-ceb78e08cf64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d1889d6-128e-5736-8a4e-4ea5163121ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334282} :answer-field/id #uuid "720ee2ab-01e8-5552-b75b-cf8b86c61c6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "159fc2f2-2f72-5d93-b610-2c9c5a129902" :question/math-academy-id "q-43807" :question/problem "Given that $x,y\\in {C}^{3},$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x,y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}i \\\\ i \\\\ i\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}i \\\\ 2 \\\\ -i\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}} \\\\  & =i\\cdot \\overset{―}{\\,i}+i\\cdot \\overset{―}{2}+i\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =i\\cdot (-i)+i\\cdot 2+i\\cdot \\text{i} \\\\  & =1+2i+(-1) \\\\  & =2i.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ae774f-2496-56c0-a800-450bd8383092" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Inner Product of Two Complex Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8faf8c5f-d1ac-514b-9d09-8a3eff6123bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334289} {:answer/id #uuid "8e774b73-c32d-582b-b065-3b6666b73855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334290} {:answer/id #uuid "51e9554d-be31-516b-bff3-ca1f2a79c123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334291} {:answer/id #uuid "04b79595-29a8-575e-9786-db29e3460d66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334292} {:answer/id #uuid "e3847b1e-09d4-5464-81e1-df9e5eac96d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3847b1e-09d4-5464-81e1-df9e5eac96d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334293} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9b97153-45c1-511f-8210-a7a683ce2aa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7e903df-f270-55b5-84eb-72ecbe8acae3" :question/math-academy-id "q-43811" :question/problem "Given that $x,y\\in {C}^{3},$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x,y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}i \\\\ 2i \\\\ -i\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1+3i \\\\ -i \\\\ -i\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}} \\\\  & =i\\cdot (\\overset{―}{1+3i})+(2i)\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-i})+(-i)\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-i}) \\\\  & =i\\cdot (1-3i)+2i\\cdot i+(-i)\\cdot \\text{i} \\\\  & =(3+i)+(-2)+1 \\\\  & =2+i.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ae774f-2496-56c0-a800-450bd8383092" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Inner Product of Two Complex Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c97cb89c-dbf7-538c-8a3b-5e08f0bdfb5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334296} {:answer/id #uuid "c55e5a72-b949-5ce2-9196-55b5846fbcc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334297} {:answer/id #uuid "7313af01-c60e-57f8-8d8a-e5bfeb21a653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334298} {:answer/id #uuid "c3178874-59ec-51e7-a921-c2b345be94ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334299} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8acddf-8a71-57ab-8196-874a7a09fe64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a8acddf-8a71-57ab-8196-874a7a09fe64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334300} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b38ac29-924a-5434-9844-bd990039dcab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03a66baa-aa45-5430-913b-f79634c2af38" :question/math-academy-id "q-43817" :question/problem "Given that $x,y\\in {C}^{3},$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x,y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-1+i \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2-i\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1+i \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}} \\\\  & =(-1+i)\\cdot (\\overset{―}{1+i})+1\\cdot \\overset{―}{3}+(2-i)\\cdot \\overset{―}{1} \\\\  & =(-1+i)\\cdot (1-i)+1\\cdot 3+(2-i)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\text{i}+3+(2-i) \\\\  & =5+i.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85ae774f-2496-56c0-a800-450bd8383092" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Inner Product of Two Complex Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a5803e9-db74-5982-b6f2-4b4d7e0862c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334303} {:answer/id #uuid "87cd1794-cf65-5d0c-98d6-18da584a818b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334304} {:answer/id #uuid "3431ee9a-01f9-5aa4-ab6e-6b0eae3c6f23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334305} {:answer/id #uuid "5a63d12c-ffba-5fb0-92d6-f582d6a52f93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-4i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334306} {:answer/id #uuid "43ca74d0-2637-5af4-8127-0c03d8f4fa2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a5803e9-db74-5982-b6f2-4b4d7e0862c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334303} :answer-field/id #uuid "46e0626f-f2c0-5dee-9e46-638685c6c00c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e7ca0b0-040c-5aeb-88ec-e41d8eb46419" :question/math-academy-id "q-71026" :question/problem "Given that $x,y\\in {C}^{4},$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x,y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}i \\\\ -i \\\\ i \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}i \\\\ 2 \\\\ -i \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}}+{x}_{4}\\overset{―}{{y}_{4}} \\\\  & =i\\cdot (\\overset{―}{\\,i})+(-i)\\cdot \\overset{―}{2}+i\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-\\text{i}})+(-1)\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-1}) \\\\  & =i\\cdot (-i)-i\\cdot 2+i\\cdot \\text{i}+(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =1-2i+(-1)+1 \\\\  & =1-2i.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5a96f74-d810-5102-837c-d10677244aa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308186} {:answer/id #uuid "627e235f-736a-57a0-8c4c-b927d8a0a834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308187} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed76953-1190-5937-83a2-bdf61fdead78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308188} {:answer/id #uuid "e55e8215-30fc-5030-967d-854ae6eaffb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308189} {:answer/id #uuid "4eeaef49-611b-536d-b874-5f989b63e2b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ed76953-1190-5937-83a2-bdf61fdead78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308188} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c239fd8-ff0e-5201-985f-8ef8c181523a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d07c4830-0431-5d0d-83f4-02741718f7fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-97149" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,a⟩>0$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $a=0$ then $⟨a,a⟩=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the inner product in ${C}^{n}$ is linear in the first argument. So, a constant multiple from the first argument can be factored out.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4509992-50c1-5828-ab5b-07250578b3a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10+8\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334310} {:answer/id #uuid "31b5d283-2a55-541e-ae49-bd7e65c9d3e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334311} {:answer/id #uuid "75364442-42c3-561f-b9ac-c519065bafd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6+8\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334312} {:answer/id #uuid "3e95a633-20e9-5d84-8b07-c79f45acfa9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5+6\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334313} {:answer/id #uuid "76940e5b-58bc-5dbd-8b37-71eeea87b239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31b5d283-2a55-541e-ae49-bd7e65c9d3e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334311} :answer-field/id #uuid "952730dc-add8-574d-835c-407db745d23a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6140addf-ac61-581c-af38-58b12a2e258d" :question/math-academy-id "q-97167" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨2a-b,\\,c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,c⟩=8+\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨c,b⟩=4-5\\text{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨2a-b,\\,c⟩ & =⟨2a,c⟩-⟨b,c⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨a,c⟩-⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2⟨a,c⟩-⟨b,c⟩ & =2⟨a,c⟩-\\overset{―}{⟨c,b}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨a,c⟩$ and $⟨c,b⟩:$    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2⟨a,c⟩-\\overset{―}{⟨c,b}⟩ & =2(8+\\text{i})-(\\overset{―}{4-5\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =2(8+\\text{i})-(4+5\\text{i}) \\\\  & =16+2\\text{i}-4-5\\text{i} \\\\  & =12-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "708e87bf-0009-52aa-a36d-60eb806f7f03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333373} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc6aff1-cee8-5a14-848d-6ce0a2bf9575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333374} {:answer/id #uuid "18a3c178-1ff7-5dbe-8f43-909f93b3cd04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333375} {:answer/id #uuid "05ac0162-68fd-579d-a436-b9959555b924" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333376} {:answer/id #uuid "6b3075fc-47fc-505e-a92d-6e8cd526f02c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05ac0162-68fd-579d-a436-b9959555b924" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333376} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0ec2b6a-0e60-5928-bcbc-9af9c861bb3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd73aa00-9e5a-5eba-8caf-b97d3032b2dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-97200" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨\\text{i}a,\\,b+c⟩$ if \n\n  $⟨a,b⟩=3-2\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨c,a⟩=2+\\text{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\text{i}a,\\,b+c⟩ & =⟨\\text{i}a,b⟩+⟨\\text{i}a,c⟩ \\\\  & =\\text{i}⟨a,b⟩+\\text{i}⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}⟨a,b⟩+\\text{i}⟨a,c⟩ & =\\text{i}⟨a,b⟩+\\text{i}\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨a,b⟩$ and $⟨c,a⟩:$    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}⟨a,b⟩+\\text{i}\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩} & =\\text{i}(3-2\\text{i})+\\text{i}\\overset{―}{(2+\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\text{i}(3-2\\text{i})+\\text{i}(2-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =2+3\\text{i}+1+2\\text{i} \\\\  & =3+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ac953-c511-5d6f-902d-4aeb804c99ce" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf518d7a-01f5-50d2-b94f-65e8a488c869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308214} {:answer/id #uuid "49b4ef0e-f1e5-54ed-ba18-bbe54250378c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308215} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f74b48-910b-58f3-8e92-bba56a87d7ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308216} {:answer/id #uuid "7719dd6e-1c7d-57be-820b-737fe5ab28e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308217} {:answer/id #uuid "9344d692-5295-53a1-bdc1-184b52e08273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9344d692-5295-53a1-bdc1-184b52e08273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+5\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308218} :answer-field/id #uuid "e34b21cc-e704-5a61-9eb7-a3fda73d7473" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa324a2f-2ed0-54ed-bd7c-536b84e60017" :question/math-academy-id "q-97209" :question/problem "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨4a-b,\\,c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨c,a⟩=1-2\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨b,c⟩=2+3\\text{i}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨4a-b,\\,c⟩ & =⟨4a,c⟩-⟨b,c⟩ \\\\  & =4⟨a,c⟩-⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4⟨a,c⟩-⟨b,c⟩ & =4\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}-⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨c,a⟩$ and $⟨b,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}-⟨b,c⟩ & =4\\overset{―}{(1-2\\text{i})}-(2+3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =4(1+2\\text{i})-(2+3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =4+8\\text{i}-2-3\\text{i} \\\\  & =2+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78ae8a6e-64c5-5c6d-9246-4ac0eb3d4eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308228} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f96905-f6cd-53ad-88cd-7c8551c2948d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308229} {:answer/id #uuid "f0551a2f-4a60-58cb-a598-26b021d36a7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308230} {:answer/id #uuid "dafb893a-2fed-57f4-b32e-01a16b0f6572" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308231} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb83b1e-f1a8-563d-b597-1ea16704e11d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0551a2f-4a60-58cb-a598-26b021d36a7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308230} :answer-field/id #uuid "84213374-f1b5-54a9-ba3b-3c509c124fda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5739baad-8a41-53ea-98cf-d058e0e17b20" :question/math-academy-id "q-97248" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,-ia⟩=-i⟨a,a⟩$ for any nonzero vector  $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, by the antilinearity in the second argument we have\n\n$$\n⟨a,-ia⟩=\\overset{―}{(-i)}⟨a,a⟩=i⟨a,a⟩\\ne -i⟨a,a⟩\n$$\n\n \nfor any nonzero vector  $a\\in {C}^{n}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4afe5cc5-6a3f-5f9c-a3fb-fa76e06d93ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308242} {:answer/id #uuid "74419b3d-b13f-57c7-9e9f-07c716d35a99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308243} {:answer/id #uuid "b3460bc8-25ee-503c-8066-b2decb3a421a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308244} {:answer/id #uuid "2dc0aed1-8969-50df-80c0-ac150ad1f5db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308245} {:answer/id #uuid "da903bef-5d47-524e-b08e-ec3f5446ac3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da903bef-5d47-524e-b08e-ec3f5446ac3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308246} :answer-field/id #uuid "49e42b05-211a-582f-9fa8-59379f469326" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6174044c-c49a-50d3-8871-9332394846b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-97296" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨a,b⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $i⟨a,b⟩=⟨a,-ib⟩$ for any  $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- There is vector $a\\in {C}^{n}$ such that  $⟨a,a⟩<0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. A complex number is equal to its conjugate if and only if it is a real number. The inner product over $C$  is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,-ib⟩=\\overset{―}{(-i)}⟨a,b⟩=i⟨a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false.  The inner product over $C$ is positive-definite. So, for any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334315 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4aa4667-1865-5528-933f-5bc88576414f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334317} {:answer/id #uuid "16ce4e59-5862-5e14-836a-f4d84baebc40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334318} {:answer/id #uuid "e4622167-cb75-5159-a99f-28399d0d21cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334319} {:answer/id #uuid "ec818b58-543a-5d33-8c6e-65bfcc04a757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334320} {:answer/id #uuid "2c1a0728-b6ce-5cc5-8627-6825e57c538a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16ce4e59-5862-5e14-836a-f4d84baebc40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334318} :answer-field/id #uuid "16991709-53aa-52e3-b461-bee5e6b8d6bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ceaeef65-5cc5-5bd2-865b-0a244110fe3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-97303" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨b,a⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨a,b⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $i⟨a,b⟩=⟨-ia,b⟩$ for any  $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is false. A complex  inner product is linear  in the first argument,  so we have \n\n$$\n⟨-ia,b⟩=-i⟨a,b⟩\\ne i⟨a,b⟩,\n$$\n\nin general.\n\n- Statement III is true. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$  $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}]}
